# CLI and generated files

Create a source-stage product from the Odroe checkout:

```sh
dart run odroe create <directory> --odroe-path <odroe-checkout>
```

Run project commands from an application that directly depends on the active
Odroe package:

```sh
dart run odroe <command> [odroe options] -- [flutter options]
```

Arguments after `--` belong to Flutter. A build target without options may be
passed directly, for example `dart run odroe build apk`.

## Commands

| Command | Purpose | Important options |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `create` | Create a guarded full-stack Flutter application | target directory, `--odroe-path` or `--odroe-version`, `--platforms`, `--org`, `--project-name`, `--offline` |
| `init` | Initialize an empty Flutter application | `--project`, `--full-stack` |
| `generate` | Generate client and server route targets | `--routes`, `--output`, `--server-output`, `--watch` |
| `dev` | Watch sources, run one selected server runtime, and optionally run Flutter | `--host`, `--port`, `--server-target`, `--sqlite-migrations`, `--server-only` |
| `build` | Build Flutter, prerendered HTML, and/or a server artifact | target and options below |

`init`, `generate`, `dev`, and `build` accept `--project`, defaulting to the
current directory.

## Creation

`create` requires one nonexistent target whose direct parent already exists.
It creates a private same-parent staging directory, runs
`flutter create --empty --no-pub`, and adds the selected Odroe dependency.
Choose exactly one source:

- `--odroe-path <checkout>` adds a path dependency. The current process confirms
  the staged package resolves the same canonical checkout and reuses its
  full-stack initializer directly.
- `--odroe-version <exact-version>` adds a hosted dependency, checks the resolved
  lockfile source and version, and runs `dart run odroe init --full-stack` from
  the staged application. Its installed package owns the templates and compiler,
  even when it differs from the package running `create`. Versions such as
  `0.1.0-dev.1` are accepted; `latest`, `any`, and ranges are rejected. The
  configured pub registry is used. This option is available in the next CLI
  revision, not in the published `0.1.0-dev.1` CLI.

The requested target is published only after all child processes and the
initializer succeed.
Initializer output is committed only after publication and names the final
target instead of its private staging path. The default platform set is
`android,ios,web`.

Validation happens before staging. A failed Flutter create, dependency
resolution, initializer, process start, or handled interrupt removes only the
private staging directory. A target that appears before publication is left
untouched. The command never overwrites an existing file, directory, or symlink
and never runs npm, migrations, builds, remote deployment, DNS, or platform
configuration changes. Dependency resolution may access the configured pub
registry by default. `--offline` is the explicit cache-only mode and requires
the selected package version and all its dependencies to be cached. An
unavailable version, failed download, or package without a compatible
`init --full-stack` command fails creation and rolls back the private stage.

## Initializers

`dart run odroe init` writes the minimal Document + Router starter.

`dart run odroe init --full-stack` writes the Flutter + Query + typed RPC +
typed SQL + SQLite + D1/Cloudflare vertical product. The generated page lists
and creates typed `Post` records instead of stopping at a scalar read. Both modes preflight all
owned paths, reject application source, and write transactionally. There is no
destructive force mode.

The small starter owns `lib/main.dart`, `lib/routes/route.dart`, and
`lib/routes/page.dart`. Full-stack mode owns these 16 application files:

```text
lib/main.dart
lib/posts.dart
lib/posts_database.dart
lib/rpc_origin.dart
lib/server.dart
lib/server_native.dart
lib/server_cloudflare.dart
lib/routes/route.dart
lib/routes/page.dart
lib/routes/server.dart
migrations/0001_posts.sql
migrations/0002_unify_posts.sql
odroe.yaml
package.json
package-lock.json
wrangler.jsonc
```

It also appends `node_modules/`, `.wrangler/`, and `.odroe/` to `.gitignore`,
then writes the generated route and bootstrap targets. Re-running an unchanged
starter is idempotent. An untouched small starter can upgrade to full-stack
mode.

## Route generation

| Option | Default |
| --- | --- |
| `--routes` | `lib/routes` |
| `--output` | `lib/routes.dart` |
| `--server-output` | `lib/routes.server.dart` |
| `--watch` | off |

`routes.dart`, `routes.server.dart`, and `.dart_tool/odroe/server.dart` begin
with Odroe ownership markers. Do not edit them. Each route directory owns a
neutral `route.dart`; optional `page.dart`, `shell.dart`, and `server.dart`
attach platform behavior.

Directories support static segments, `[name]` parameters, `[...name]` rest
parameters, and pathless `(group)` segments. `route.dart` exposes one top-level
`AppRoute<Params, Search, Data>` named `route`. Named-record parameter and
search schemas generate codecs for `String`, `int`, `double`, `bool`, and
`List<String>`; custom field types require an explicit codec.

Public top-level `ServerFunction<Input, Output>` declarations in `server.dart`
become generated client references. Give released functions an explicit,
stable `id`. A project-local named-record typedef imported with a prefix also
receives symmetric client-input, server-input, server-output, and client-output
codecs. A directly declared project enum uses the same four boundaries and is
transported by case name, including through aliases, supported collections,
records, and streams. Unsupported positional, generic, recursive, indirect, or
private shapes fail generation instead of failing on the first request.

Cloudflare builds also own `.dart_tool/odroe/server_fetch.dart`. Applications
may provide `lib/server.dart` with `createServer()` and `lib/prerender.dart`
with `prerenderLocations()`; those two files remain application source.

## Development

`dev` defaults to `127.0.0.1:3000`. `--server-only` does not start Flutter.
Otherwise pass the device and Flutter flags after `--`:

```sh
dart run odroe dev -- -d chrome
```

The default `--server-target native` runs the Dart IO server and retains the
Flutter integration above.

For Native development, `odroe.yaml` can select one project-relative SQLite
history:

```yaml
sqlite_migrations: migrations
```

This is the only supported key; unknown keys and unsafe paths fail before the
command starts. Odroe passes the resolved directory through
`ODROE_MIGRATIONS_PATH` and watches its top-level `.sql` files. A change
regenerates routes and restarts the Native server, which validates and applies
pending files before listening. `--sqlite-migrations <path>` overrides the
config for one command. Native development resolves
`--sqlite-migrations` → inherited `ODROE_MIGRATIONS_PATH` → `odroe.yaml` in
that order. Cloudflare development ignores the config and rejects the CLI
option; D1 migrations remain an explicit Wrangler command.

Cloudflare development is intentionally an explicit server-only path:

```sh
npm ci
dart run odroe build --no-server web
dart run odroe dev --server-target cloudflare --server-only
```

It requires application-owned `wrangler.jsonc` and
the project-local `node_modules/wrangler` package. The starter's config serves
`build/web`, so the explicit assets build above is required on a clean project
and after Flutter UI or asset changes. Odroe generates routes, compiles
`build/odroe/cloudflare/server.js` through a temporary sibling, atomically
replaces that server JavaScript, and starts `wrangler dev --local` with the
current `worker.mjs` as an explicit entrypoint. This prevents a stale configured
entrypoint from silently surviving a Dart source change.

Changes under `lib/` regenerate routes. Server-relevant changes compile a new
Worker and let Wrangler reload it without restarting the CLI. Initial route
generation and compilation must succeed before Wrangler starts. After that,
invalid route or Dart source never replaces the last-known-good Worker; the
compiler reports the failure and the watcher retries after the next edit. This
mode does not apply D1 migrations, build Flutter Web, or deploy. Those remain
explicit commands.

## Build options

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--server-only` | off | Build no Flutter target or static output |
| `--[no-]server` | on | Emit or skip a deployable server artifact |
| `--server-target` | `native` | `native` or `cloudflare` |
| `--server-artifact` | target-specific | Native bundle root or Cloudflare JavaScript path inside the project build directory |
| `--sqlite-migrations` | `odroe.yaml`, otherwise off | One-command SQLite source override inside the project |
| `--[no-]prerender` | on | Generate static HTML where applicable |
| `--prerender-output` | `build/web` | Static output inside the project |
| `--prerender-concurrency` | `4` | Parallel prerender requests |
| `--prerender-crawl` | off | Discover same-origin, query-free HTML links |
| `--prerender-max-routes` | `1000` | Explicit plus discovered route ceiling |
| `--prerender-max-response-bytes` | `1048576` | HTML bytes allowed per route |

For Native, `--server-artifact` names the complete bundle root, not the
executable. It defaults to `build/odroe/server`:

```text
build/odroe/server/
├── .odroe-native-bundle
├── bin/
│   └── server
├── lib/
└── migrations/  # when SQLite history is selected
```

Invoke `bin/server` from any working directory, and deploy the whole directory
so native libraries keep their relative location. The generated bootstrap
validates the ownership marker and selects the bundle root before application
creation. A compiled entry rejects a missing, non-regular, or changed marker;
non-product source and development runs keep their project working directory
without requiring one. CLI-managed prerender validates the compiled bundle,
then retains the project directory for build-time source resources. Windows
emits `bin/server.exe` with the same contract.
Cloudflare keeps a file contract: it writes
`build/odroe/cloudflare/server.js` and `worker.mjs`. Static Document and Flutter
output lives under `build/web` unless changed explicitly.

`odroe.yaml` makes the application's persistent selection explicit;
`--sqlite-migrations <path>` overrides it for one build. Either selection makes
a Native build validate those `NNNN_snake_case.sql` files and emit a clean
`migrations/` directory inside the selected bundle root. With neither, Odroe
does not reserve a generic source path used by PostgreSQL or MySQL projects.
The copy is byte-for-byte and nonrecursive;
invalid names, duplicate versions, symbolic links, and a non-directory target
fail the build. A hidden ownership marker protects the complete bundle. Odroe
refuses to replace an unmarked output. If neither config nor CLI selects a
history, a later build also refuses to replace a bundle that already contains
migrations. Cloudflare-only builds ignore `odroe.yaml` because Wrangler reads
the application directory directly; passing `--sqlite-migrations` there is an
error. A legacy standalone executable is also
preserved rather than guessed into this layout; after retaining its migration
history, move or remove that build output explicitly before the first bundle
build.

Route source cannot overlap either generated target or any destructive build
output. Migration source, generated route targets, server bundle roots,
Cloudflare artifacts, and prerender output also must not overlap. Odroe
validates those paths before writing generated code or replacing build output,
and serializes complete Native bundle publication across processes. The
selected Flutter target output, including an explicit forwarded
`--output`/`--output-dir` and the effective `flutter config --build-dir`, is
reserved from those Odroe sources and server artifacts as well.

For native prerender, `build` overrides the child server's
`ODROE_SQLITE_PATH` with a temporary database path and removes that directory
after shutdown. When `odroe.yaml` or `--sqlite-migrations` selects a history,
the build also pins `ODROE_MIGRATIONS_PATH`. A Native artifact prerenders from
its published bundle snapshot; assets-only and Cloudflare builds prerender from
the validated project source. It does not pass the temporary database override
when users run `bin/server` from the deployable bundle.

The application owns `pubspec.yaml`, `odroe.yaml`, platform hosts, `public/`,
migrations, secrets, `wrangler.jsonc`, container files, and provider deployment
policy.
Odroe never deploys as a side effect of `build`.
